::ENG2_U06_L05_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] A slide heading says, "Stuff We Found." Which revision best improves clarity for a digital presentation about the student survey? {
~A. Survey Things #Incorrect. This heading is still vague. It does not tell what the survey showed or why the information matters.
=B. Survey Evidence Shows Students Need Supervised Space #Correct. This heading tells the audience the topic, the evidence source, and the main finding. A clear heading prepares the viewer to understand the slide.
~C. Our Project #Incorrect. This heading identifies that a project exists, but it does not help the audience understand the specific finding.
~D. A Colorful Student Slide #Incorrect. This focuses on appearance instead of the information the audience needs to understand.
}

::ENG2_U06_L05_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which detail is the strongest evidence to include on a slide about the need for an after-school study space? {
~A. Some students probably stay after school. #Incorrect. This is a guess. Digital presentations need evidence that can be traced to a source or data point.
~B. Everyone knows students like places to study. #Incorrect. This is a broad claim without support. It does not provide verifiable evidence.
~C. A study space would be nice. #Incorrect. This is an opinion, not evidence. It does not show why the solution is needed.
=D. 68 of 120 surveyed students remain on campus after dismissal. #Correct. This is specific, measurable evidence. It helps the audience understand the size of the need.
}

::ENG2_U06_L05_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] A slide has many tiny numbers on a busy background. What should the presenter do first to meet the audience's needs? {
=A. Keep the most important numbers and label them in a readable chart. #Correct. This keeps the evidence while making it easier for the audience to understand.
~B. Add more colors so the slide looks more interesting. #Incorrect. More decoration can make the information harder to read.
~C. Remove all numbers so the slide is shorter. #Incorrect. Removing evidence weakens the presentation. The goal is to present evidence clearly, not erase it.
~D. Leave the slide alone because the speaker can explain it. #Incorrect. In a digital presentation, the slide should support understanding even before the speaker explains.
}

::ENG2_U06_L05_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which phrase uses the most precise academic vocabulary for explaining a survey result? {
~A. a bunch of stuff #Incorrect. This phrase is too informal and unclear for an academic presentation.
~B. some things #Incorrect. This phrase is vague. It does not tell the audience what kind of information is being discussed.
=C. credible evidence #Correct. This phrase accurately names trustworthy information used to support a claim.
~D. cool details #Incorrect. This phrase may sound positive, but it is not precise enough for academic communication.
}

::ENG2_U06_L05_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Why should a presenter avoid a bright visual that makes the data hard to read? {
~A. Digital presentations should never use visuals. #Incorrect. Visuals can help when they clarify information.
=B. A visual that hides evidence distracts from the message. #Correct. The purpose of a digital visual is to help the audience understand evidence, not compete with it.
~C. All slides must use only black and white. #Incorrect. Color can be useful when it improves organization and readability.
~D. The audience should only listen to the speaker. #Incorrect. The digital presentation should work with the speaker to support understanding.
}
